  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sudarsanan vs The Food Inspector on 13 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Wednesday, The 13Th Day Of January 2016/23Rd Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 3956 of 2008 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 35/2001 of Sessions Court,Thiruvananthapuram Cc 476/1995 of J.M.F.C.-Ii,Thiruvananthapuram Revision Petitioner(S)/Appellant-1St Accused:: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- Sudarsanan, S/O.Bhaskaran, Kuzhivilakomveedu, Nanavila P.O., Thiruvananthapuram. By Adv. Sri.Millu Dandapani Respondent(S)/Counter Petitioner-Complainant And State: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. The Food Inspector, Thiruvananthapuram Circle. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. R1 & R2 By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 13-01-2016 , The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.3956 of 2008 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 13th day of January 2016 Sudarsanan vs The Food Inspector on 13 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/69932090/ 1 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 21.7.1995 at about 12 noon, PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased 750 grams of peas dhal from the shop of the first accused. Second accused was the supplier of the food item. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P9 report stating that the sample contained 28.1% of kesari dhal and therefore, the same was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW6 were examined and Exts. P1 to P16 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 to DW4 were examined and Exts.D1 and D2 were marked for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P9 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P9 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. Even otherwise, in view of the decision in Pepsico India Holdings (supra), the conviction in this case cannot be sustained as no laboratory was notified and no validated method of Sudarsanan vs The Food Inspector on 13 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/69932090/ 2 analysis was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P9 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P9 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. Even otherwise, in view of the decision in Pepsico India Holdings (supra), the conviction in this case cannot be sustained as no laboratory was notified and no validated method of Sudarsanan vs The Food Inspector on 13 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/69932090/ 2 analysis was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954."
